window.L2_DATA={chapters:[
 {id:'zero',title:'The power of zero',place:'The pattern machine',icon:'x⁰',story:'A machine divides by the same number each time. Follow 8, 4, 2, 1 to discover why a nonzero number to the power zero is 1.',why:'Powers help describe repeated growth and shrinking. A zero exponent is the starting point before any multiplication steps.',steps:[['Follow the pattern','2³ = 8 → 2² = 4 → 2¹ = 2 → 2⁰ = 1. Each step divides by 2.'],['Keep the exception','For x ≠ 0, x⁰ = 1. We leave 0⁰ undefined in these lessons. The rule cannot be justified by dividing by zero.'],['Simple equation','x⁰ + 4 = 5 becomes 1 + 4 = 5. Every nonzero real x works; there is no single answer for x.'],['Two placeholders','3x⁰ + 2y⁰ + z = 10, with x ≠ 0 and y ≠ 0, becomes 3 + 2 + z = 10. Subtract 5: z = 5.']]},
 {id:'linear',title:'Keep the balance',place:'The playground café',icon:'x¹',story:'A snack costs x rupees. Add a ₹5 drink and the bill is ₹12. An equation is a way to name the price you do not know.',why:'Use equations to work backwards from a bill, share a budget, or work out a journey time.',steps:[['Name the unknown','x is the snack price. x¹ means x: one copy of the number. The bill gives x + 5 = 12.'],['Undo on both sides','Subtract ₹5 from BOTH sides: x + 5 − 5 = 12 − 5. So x = 7. A balanced equation stays balanced.'],['Check the answer','Put 7 back into the bill: 7 + 5 = 12. The two sides match.'],['Two unknown prices','2x + 3y = 12 has many possible pairs. If x = 3 is supplied, 6 + 3y = 12 → 3y = 6 → y = 2. One equation alone does not determine both prices.']]},
 {id:'square',title:'Build a square garden',place:'The tile workshop',icon:'x²',story:'A square garden is x metres long and x metres wide. Its area is x × x. How long is a side if the garden covers 16 square metres?',why:'Squares describe area. Factoring helps find which values make a more complicated expression zero.',steps:[['Rows × columns','x² means x × x, not 2 × x. A side of 4 m gives 4 × 4 = 16 m².'],['Two algebra answers','x² = 16 has real solutions x = 4 and x = −4, because (−4)(−4) = 16. A garden side must be positive, so the garden uses 4 m.'],['Factor the challenge','x² + 5x + 6 = (x + 2)(x + 3). Why? x² + 3x + 2x + 6 = x² + 5x + 6. The two numbers add to 5 and multiply to 6.'],['Use the zero-product rule','If (x + 2)(x + 3) = 0, at least one factor must be zero. x + 2 = 0 or x + 3 = 0, so x = −2 or −3. Check each in the original equation.']]},
 {id:'cube',title:'Stack a storage cube',place:'The building yard',icon:'x³',story:'Stack little cubes into a box that is x units wide, deep and tall. Each layer has x² cubes, and there are x layers.',why:'Cubes describe volume: how much space a box holds. Equations help work backwards from capacity to a missing dimension.',steps:[['Three dimensions','x³ = x × x × x. At x = 3, each layer has 9 cubes and 3 layers hold 27 cubes.'],['Work backwards','x³ = 27 gives x = 3 among real numbers. A negative number cubed stays negative: (−3)³ = −27.'],['Take out the shared part','x³ − 2x² = 0 becomes x²(x − 2) = 0. Both terms contain x².'],['Do not lose zero','x² = 0 or x − 2 = 0 gives x = 0 or x = 2. Dividing the original equation by x² would lose the valid solution x = 0.']]},
 {id:'angles',title:'Turn, open, rotate',place:'The playground gate',icon:'∠',story:'Open a gate from its closed position. The angle measures the turn around its hinge, not the length of the gate.',why:'Angles describe turns on a route, door openings, ramps, clock hands and the corners of buildings.',steps:[['Start at the hinge','The meeting point is the vertex. The two rays show the start and end directions.'],['Small and square turns','An acute angle is between 0° and 90°. A right angle is exactly 90°.'],['Keep turning','An obtuse angle is between 90° and 180°. A straight angle is 180°. A reflex angle is between 180° and 360°.'],['Finish the circle','A full turn is 360°. A zero angle is 0°. Longer rays do not change the angle. The marked arc tells you which turn to measure.']]},
 {id:'draw',title:'Draw it yourself',place:'The architect’s desk',icon:'✎',story:'Design a path that turns through 60°. Use a virtual protractor, then try the same construction with a ruler and protractor on paper.',why:'Builders and designers need accurate directions. Measuring from the correct zero mark prevents drawing the supplementary angle by mistake.',steps:[['Draw the baseline','The blue ray points right from the marked vertex. It is your 0° direction.'],['Align the protractor','Its centre is on the vertex and its 0° mark lies on the baseline. Use the scale starting from the right.'],['Mark the angle','Tap or drag on the board at your chosen degree. Read the angle from the right-hand zero, going anticlockwise.'],['Connect and check','Press Connect my ray. A second ray joins the vertex to your mark. Check against the target, then hide the protractor and try a new angle.']]},
 {id:'missing',title:'Find the missing turn',place:'The path junction',icon:'x°',story:'Paths meet at a junction. Use the total turn to work out a missing angle without measuring the drawing.',why:'A known geometric rule becomes an equation. Collect what you know, subtract it from the total, then check.',steps:[['Straight line','110° + x = 180°, so x = 70°. The two adjacent angles complete a half turn.'],['Around a point','120° + 150° + x = 360°, so x = 90°. A complete turn contains all the sectors.'],['Crossing paths','Vertically opposite angles are equal. If one angle is 45°, the opposite one is 45°. Its neighbour is 180° − 45° = 135°.'],['With algebra','2x + 3x + 50° = 180° → 5x + 50° = 180° → 5x = 130° → x = 26°. The actual angles are 52°, 78° and 50°.']]},
 {id:'quads',title:'Design a four-sided park',place:'The park planning table',icon:'▱',story:'Connect four corners to outline a park. A diagonal splits a simple quadrilateral into two triangles: 180° + 180° = 360°.',why:'Angle and side properties help identify shapes and plan frames, tiles, plots and roofs. Appearance alone can be misleading.',steps:[['Four corners, one total','The interior angles of a simple, non-crossing quadrilateral total 360°. In a concave shape, one interior angle is reflex.'],['Square and rectangle','Both have four right angles. A square also has four equal sides; a rectangle need not.'],['Parallel sides','A parallelogram has opposite angles equal and neighbouring angles adding to 180°. A rhombus is a parallelogram with four equal sides; it need not have right angles.'],['Trapezium and kite','Here, trapezium (trapezoid) means a shape with a pair of parallel sides. Interior angles along each leg add to 180°. A kite has two pairs of adjacent equal sides; the opposite angles between unequal sides are equal.'],['Solve the missing corner','100° + 100° + 80° + x = 360° → 280° + x = 360° → x = 80°. Always check the total and any extra shape properties.']]}
],questions:[]};
(function(){const q=(topic,prompt,answer,options,steps)=>L2_DATA.questions.push({topic,prompt,answer,options:[answer,...options],steps});
q('zero','For x = 7, what is x⁰?', '1',['0','7','Undefined'],['7 is nonzero. Every nonzero number to power zero is 1.']);
q('zero','Which x values satisfy x⁰ + 4 = 5 in these lessons?','Every nonzero real x',['Only 1','Only 0','Every real x, including 0'],['For nonzero x, x⁰ = 1.','1 + 4 = 5 is true. We do not define 0⁰ here.']);
q('zero','3x⁰ + 2y⁰ + z = 10, with x and y nonzero. Find z.','5',['10','0','1'],['3(1) + 2(1) + z = 10.','5 + z = 10, so z = 5.']);
q('zero','How do we treat 0⁰ in this course?','Undefined',['Always 1','Always 0','Always 10'],['The nonzero-base rule does not apply to zero.','We leave 0⁰ undefined in these lessons.']);
q('linear','A snack and a ₹5 drink cost ₹12. What is the snack price?','₹7',['₹17','₹5','₹12'],['x + 5 = 12.','Subtract 5 from both sides: x = 7.']);
q('linear','Solve 3x = 18.','6',['15','21','54'],['Three equal groups total 18.','Divide both sides by 3: x = 6.']);
q('linear','2x + 3y = 12. If x = 3, find y.','2',['3','6','4'],['2(3) + 3y = 12.','3y = 6, so y = 2.']);
q('linear','Does 2x + 3y = 12 alone determine one unique pair?','No, many pairs work',['Yes, only (3, 2)','Yes, only (0, 4)','No pairs work'],['(3, 2) gives 6 + 6 = 12.','(0, 4) also gives 0 + 12 = 12.']);
q('square','Find ALL real solutions of x² = 16.','4 and −4',['4 only','−4 only','8 and −8'],['4 × 4 = 16.','(−4) × (−4) = 16 too.']);
q('square','A square garden has area 25 m². What is its side length?','5 m',['−5 m','25 m','10 m'],['Side × side = area. 5 × 5 = 25.','A physical side length must be positive.']);
q('square','Factor x² + 5x + 6.','(x + 2)(x + 3)',['(x + 1)(x + 6)','(x − 2)(x − 3)','x(x + 5)'],['2 + 3 = 5 and 2 × 3 = 6.','Expand to check: x² + 3x + 2x + 6.']);
q('square','Solve x² + 5x + 6 = 0.','−2 and −3',['2 and 3','0 and 6','−1 and −6'],['(x + 2)(x + 3) = 0.','One factor is zero, so x = −2 or x = −3.']);
q('cube','Solve x³ = 27 over the real numbers.','3',['9','3 and −3','27'],['3 × 3 × 3 = 27.','(−3)³ is −27, not 27.']);
q('cube','A cube has side length 4 cm. What is its volume?','64 cm³',['16 cm³','12 cm³','8 cm³'],['One layer has 4 × 4 = 16 unit cubes.','Four layers have 16 × 4 = 64.']);
q('cube','Factor x³ − 2x².','x²(x − 2)',['x(x − 2)','x²(x + 2)','x(x² − 2x²)'],['Both terms contain x².','x² × x − x² × 2 = x³ − 2x².']);
q('cube','Find ALL real solutions of x³ − 2x² = 0.','0 and 2',['2 only','0 only','−2 and 2'],['x²(x − 2) = 0.','x² = 0 gives 0; x − 2 = 0 gives 2.']);
q('angles','A gate opens through 65°. What type of angle is it?','Acute',['Obtuse','Right','Reflex'],['65° is greater than 0° and less than 90°.']);
q('angles','A turn measures 240°. What type is it?','Reflex',['Obtuse','Straight','Acute'],['240° is greater than 180° and less than 360°.']);
q('angles','A quarter turn is how many degrees?','90°',['45°','180°','360°'],['A full turn is 360°.','360 ÷ 4 = 90°.']);
q('angles','Making both rays longer changes the angle by how much?','0°',['90°','180°','It doubles'],['Angle measures the turn between directions.','Changing ray length does not change the turn.']);
q('draw','Where does the centre of the protractor go?','On the vertex',['At the end of the longer ray','Anywhere on the baseline','At the 90° mark'],['The vertex is the point where the rays meet.','Align the protractor centre with it.']);
q('draw','Your baseline points right. Which zero do you start reading from?','The right-hand 0°',['The left-hand 0°','90°','180°'],['Choose the scale whose zero matches your baseline.']);
q('draw','You accidentally draw 120° instead of 60°. What is a likely reason?','You read the opposite scale',['You made the ray too long','You used a sharp pencil','You marked the vertex'],['The same position can show 60 and 120 on opposite scales.','Start from the zero on your baseline.']);
q('draw','After marking 60°, what completes the angle?','Connect the vertex to the mark',['Join the two ray tips','Erase the vertex','Draw a circle'],['A ray from the vertex through the marked point sets the second direction.']);
q('missing','On a straight line, 110° + x = 180°. Find x.','70°',['250°','110°','90°'],['x = 180° − 110° = 70°.']);
q('missing','Around a point: 120° + 150° + x = 360°. Find x.','90°',['30°','180°','270°'],['Known angles total 270°.','x = 360° − 270° = 90°.']);
q('missing','Two lines cross. One angle is 45°. Its vertically opposite angle is…','45°',['135°','90°','315°'],['Vertically opposite angles are equal.','The neighbouring angles are 135°.']);
q('missing','On a line: 2x + 3x + 50° = 180°. Find x.','26°',['52°','78°','130°'],['5x + 50° = 180°.','5x = 130°, so x = 26°.']);
q('quads','A quadrilateral has angles 100°, 100°, 80° and x. Find x.','80°',['100°','180°','280°'],['Known total = 280°.','x = 360° − 280° = 80°.']);
q('quads','One parallelogram angle is 65°. What is its neighbouring angle?','115°',['65°','25°','295°'],['Consecutive angles between parallel sides total 180°.','180° − 65° = 115°.']);
q('quads','Which statement about a rhombus is always true?','All four sides are equal',['All angles are 90°','Only one pair of sides is equal','It cannot be a square'],['A rhombus has four equal sides.','A square is a special rhombus with four right angles.']);
q('quads','Why do a simple quadrilateral’s interior angles total 360°?','It splits into two triangles',['Every angle must be 90°','Its sides are equal','It has a circle inside'],['Draw an interior diagonal to split it into two triangles.','Each triangle totals 180°; together they total 360°.']);
})();
